Step 3: Add createPrediction

We'll implement this as an async function that:

receives the form data,
creates a prediction,
saves it,
redirects back to the homepage.

We'll also use a try...catch block so that database errors are handled properly.

Why we're doing it this way

Notice how the responsibilities are now clearly separated:

homeController
    ↓
Displays homepage

predictionController
    ↓
Creates, updates and deletes predictions

predictionModel
    ↓
Defines the database structure

Before we write the function...

I want to settle one architectural decision.

Your form currently contains these fields:

country
league
homeTeam
awayTeam
prediction
odds
confidence
status
kickoffTime
isPremium
result

Your schema also has:

createdBy: {
    type: mongoose.Schema.Types.ObjectId,
    ref: "User"
}

Why createPrediction?

It follows a common MVC naming convention:

Action	Controller Function
Show homepage	index
Show form	newPrediction
Create prediction	createPrediction
Show one prediction	showPrediction
Show edit form	editPrediction
Update prediction	updatePrediction
Delete prediction	deletePrediction

This makes your code self-explanatory and easy to extend.

